<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The Handle ~ a quorum-presented name, recognised by its own key

A ''Handle'' names a face the world may recognise again — the Dread Pirate Roberts, Guru-Josh, Luther
Blissett. Two modules stand it: a ''handle-card'' publishes ONE face (its key, a display glamour, pointers
to where its standing lives) and certifies itself; a ''handle-KEL'' carries that face's key-event-log — the
chain the card points into. A human keeps many unlinkable Handles in a private vault (persona-circle
#the-vault); publishing one reveals nothing of the others, because they share no key and the collector that
knows them one human is never published.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-mu>>

!! The k-of-n HandleGlamour ~ a Handle is a quorum-presented name, not a lone key (operator, 2026-09-08)

The owner-binding generalises from a single owner key to a ''presenting owner-set'' — the member persona
prefixes plus a graft threshold. A public Handle reads VALID against its CURRENT set, and whether ONE human
or MANY hold the right to present it stays ''unknowable by design'', exactly as a cabal refuses "which member
signed".

# ''1-of-1 is the degenerate case that IS the personal face.'' The same shape all the way down — no special
civic tier. `mintHandleInception` keeps the single-owner path ergonomic as sugar over a one-member set.
# ''The field's own vocabulary (Deseriis, improper names).'' A many-present name is a MULTIPLE-USE NAME
(Luther Blissett — hundreds shared one, then retired it); a chartered set is a COLLECTIVE PSEUDONYM (Wu Ming
— a controlled group). The 1-of-1-that-many-present and the k-of-n-chartered ARE those two forms.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/self-certifying>>

!! The card certifies ITSELF ~ the name contains the key

The card is signed by the Handle's OWN key, and that key rides IN the card (`nym`). A recogniser verifies the
signature against the embedded key and ''needs no directory to trust'' (Mazières' self-certifying names;
did:key; a Nostr npub). Zooko's triangle resolves: the identifier stands secure-and-decentralised because it
IS the key, and memorable because each recogniser keeps a ''LOCAL petname'' for it. No SIN, no global registry
to capture.

''Recognition runs in TWO steps, and conflating them is the classic error.'' First the card must certify
itself — a valid signature by the key it names (`verifyHandleCard`). Then the recogniser asks whether that
key is the one their petname points at (`recognizeHandle`). A card can certify itself perfectly and STILL be
a stranger; only the local petname turns a valid key into "the mover who healed Neo-Thracia". The petname
lives in the recogniser's own book, never on any wire.

''A rejection NAMES itself.'' `CardRejection` distinguishes wrong-domain · malformed · bad-signature ·
expired · wrong-nym · rollback · lineage-break. A recogniser that learns only "invalid" cannot tell a forgery
from a lapsed lease from a typo, and re-presents blind.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/monotone-leased-lineage>>

!! Monotone, leased, lineage-linked ~ a face carried over time

The card carries a monotone `version` (a stale card cannot roll a fresh one back), a `prev` link (the
recogniser follows one face's history), and an `expiry` (a lease read against the recogniser's LOCAL clock —
an unfed card goes stale on its own, since a negative fact cannot be made to arrive). The word is
deliberately not `epoch`: that names the mesh's fencing frontier, never a wall-clock.

''Identity and signature answer different questions, so they cover different bytes.'' The card's IDENTITY
content (`handleCardIdBytes`) leaves `expiry` OUT, so a renewal — the same face, a fresh lease — keeps the
SAME card id and the `prev` lineage stays stable across heartbeats. The SIGNED content (`handleCardBytes`)
folds `expiry` IN, so no one extends anyone's lease by editing a number the signer never covered. One
function cannot serve both; two do.

''The announce reader rule'' (`acceptHandleUpdate`) accepts the NEWER face and refuses a rollback or a fork:
it carries the anti-rollback-by-version and anti-equivocation-by-prev discipline `oracle-substrate` proves
for its pointer, applied to the card's own monotone fields. Idempotent replay of the current card passes
under gossip/merge; only a card that PURPORTS to advance is held to the lineage link.

''A face may prove it speaks for a FLEET.'' An optional `fleetProof` — a delegation edge the persona root
signed over this nym — lets a recogniser walk nym → root in one extra verify. Absent, the card certifies only
itself, which stays complete and honest: a face that claims no fleet claims nothing false. The root signs the
nym before the card exists, so no circle forms.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-chain>>

!! The handle-KEL ~ a SIBLING grammar to the persona-KEL, never an extension

The handle-KEL reuses the frozen persona-KEL's PRIMITIVES by shape — hash-linked events, a content-addressed
cid, a prefix derived over the inception, an ARMED inception carrying a rolling recovery commitment — and
mints its OWN kinds the persona grammar lacks.

# ''The owner-binding folds a DIGEST of the GENESIS owner-set into the prefix'' (`sealKeySetHash` over the
members + graft threshold), so the founding quorum is fixed in the name FOREVER (anti-swap: the name never
moves) and the handle↔identity proof never detaches. Alongside that fixed genesis wall, each event carries a
ROLLING `ownerSetHash` — the CURRENT presenting-set digest — separate from the rolling recovery commitment.
# ''ROTATION · BURN · ATTEST are authorized by ANY CURRENT MEMBER.'' An event names which member presents
and that member's head op-key; verify checks the member stands in the current owner set AND that an injected
`OwnerHeadResolver` confirms the key is that member's authoritative head.
# ''BURN is terminal'' — the Shadowtalk ending made structural. A buried name attests nothing; nothing rotates
or grafts past it. A burn signs either by the seated Handle key (self-burn) or by a current member (owner-burn).
# ''GRAFT is the succession move'' — the presenting owner-set turns over. It reveals the NEW current set and
carries authorization from the PRIOR set, reaching that set's THRESHOLD: k of the current n consent, gathered
as the presenter plus `graftSigs` co-signers — each a distinct current member signing the same graft bytes
(the persona-KEL QuorumSignature shape). A 1-of-1 grafts by one willing hand (Roberts cedes to Westley); a
k-of-n guild refuses a one-hand graft and accepts a k-hand one. `verifyHandleKel` counts distinct prior-set
authorizers against the prior threshold (structural), `verifyHandleKelFull` proves each signature + head.
# ''ATTEST is a signed claim carried ON the card, never an event and never a board.'' A claim ("this Handle
controls example.net") binds the head event cid, so a rotation/graft/burn STALES it and the fresh head
re-attests. A domain-attestation travels on the card and verifies reader-locally against the empire's own
registry (DNS); this stack MUST NOT compile a Handle→domain index, ever (registry-filter).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/two-tiers>>

!! Two verification tiers ~ self-contained structure ⊥ board-checked presentation

The chain answers at two assurance depths, and they cross different ground:

* ''Tier-1 — self-contained'' (`verifyHandleKel`): sequence, hash-link, prefix, owner-binding, membership,
burn-terminality, and cid all recompute from the chain's OWN bytes. No injection, no board, no network — a
recogniser holding the chain settles structure alone. `verifyAttestation` and `verifyHandleCard` ride here.
* ''Tier-2 — board-checked presentation authority'' (`verifyHandleKelFull`): every rotation and graft
`authSig` must verify AND its signer must stand as the named member's authoritative head, per the injected
`OwnerHeadResolver` — a presentation signed by a SUPERSEDED member key refuses HERE. That resolver typically
walks each member's persona-KEL to its verified head; a verifier holding the members' chain snapshots
(carried beside the card) resolves reader-locally, one without them needs the members' boards. ''The
resolver NAMES that dependency instead of hiding it.''

Both fail CLOSED on the first break. The tiers are the ONE seam where the sibling grammar touches the persona
plane, kept as an interface so this module never imports the persona-KEL.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/public-by-design>>

!! Public by design ~ one chain per call, no registry surface

A handle-KEL diffuses wherever the card announces — the persona-KEL's per-Nexus board discipline does NOT
govern it. Accordingly the module handles ONE chain per call and exposes NO surface accepting a collection of
others' Handles. A compiled index over self-announced cards — however helpful its face — is the rejected
registry; the cards pass, the compilation does not (registry-filter).

Pure and isomorphic: the module holds no I/O and no key. The vessel supplies the signer; the caller carries
the bytes; the read-open oracle plane serves the published blob.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

